WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.467
Security enforcement continues after
fields, identity,

2
00:00:03.547 --> 00:00:05.302
and permission have been checked.

3
00:00:05.382 --> 00:00:08.470
The server also owns workflow and
shared-state invariants.

4
00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:10.790
A rejected item may no longer be
approvable.

5
00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:12.807
A benefit may be redeemable only once.

6
00:00:12.887 --> 00:00:16.822
Inventory cannot be reserved after another
request consumes the final unit.

7
00:00:16.902 --> 00:00:20.959
A record displayed in the browser may
change before the user's request arrives.

8
00:00:21.039 --> 00:00:23.824
Client-side state describes an earlier
observation;

9
00:00:23.904 --> 00:00:26.689
it cannot enforce current state shared by
all callers.

10
00:00:26.769 --> 00:00:30.704
A disabled button after submission does
not prevent a script from sending the

11
00:00:30.784 --> 00:00:33.387
request twice, two tabs from acting
concurrently,

12
00:00:33.467 --> 00:00:35.949
or a network retry from repeating the
operation.

13
00:00:36.029 --> 00:00:39.662
Where safe repetition is required, define
idempotency deliberately.

14
00:00:39.742 --> 00:00:43.314
An idempotency key can associate retries
with one logical operation,

15
00:00:43.394 --> 00:00:46.421
but the server must persist and enforce
that relationship.

16
00:00:46.501 --> 00:00:48.014
For naturally unique events,

17
00:00:48.094 --> 00:00:52.090
a database uniqueness constraint may be
stronger than an in-memory flag on one

18
00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:53.018
server process.

19
00:00:53.098 --> 00:00:57.336
Consider the interval between checking a
condition and changing protected state.

20
00:00:57.416 --> 00:01:00.746
Code that reads available inventory,
confirms a positive count,

21
00:01:00.826 --> 00:01:05.063
and later writes a decrement contains a
race when another request can change the row

22
00:01:05.143 --> 00:01:06.354
between those actions.

23
00:01:06.434 --> 00:01:10.067
The check and change may need a
transaction, conditional update, lock,

24
00:01:10.147 --> 00:01:12.508
optimistic concurrency with a version
field,

25
00:01:12.588 --> 00:01:16.523
or a database operation that succeeds only
while the invariant remains true.

26
00:01:16.603 --> 00:01:18.662
The mechanism depends on the data store;

27
00:01:18.742 --> 00:01:22.011
application validation alone does not
eliminate concurrency.

28
00:01:22.091 --> 00:01:25.360
Transactions group changes that must
succeed or fail together,

29
00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:29.073
while database constraints protect
invariants against every writer.

30
00:01:29.153 --> 00:01:33.088
Application services still provide
readable policy and meaningful errors.

31
00:01:33.168 --> 00:01:37.587
Handle transaction conflicts and
constraint violations as expected outcomes rather

32
00:01:37.667 --> 00:01:40.331
than assuming an earlier check guaranteed
success.

33
00:01:40.411 --> 00:01:43.378
The engineering sequence is to authorize
the transition,

34
00:01:43.458 --> 00:01:47.575
verify authoritative state, and make the
protected change atomically whenever

35
00:01:47.655 --> 00:01:50.500
concurrent requests could otherwise
violate the rule.
